<span class="kicker">2026-07-27 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Build a Paver Walkway on a Slope</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Building a paver walkway on level ground is forgiving. Building one on a slope is a different project entirely. Water moves differently, soil shifts differently, and a base that would hold up fine on flat ground can wash out or settle unevenly once gravity gets involved. None of that means it can’t be done well, it just means the margin for error is much smaller than it is on a flat path.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we build sloped walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and slope is one of the first things we assess before recommending an approach. This guide covers the two main ways to handle a sloped walkway, stepped or gradual, along with the drainage, erosion, cost, and material considerations that make or break a sloped installation over time. If you’re starting from scratch and want the fundamentals first, our general guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway/"> how to build a paver walkway</a> covers the base steps that still apply here, just adapted for a grade.</p>
<h2><b>Stepped Walkway or Gradual Slope: Which Approach Fits Your Yard</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Stepped Walkway</b></td><td><b>Gradual Sloped Walkway</b></td></tr><tr><td>Best for slope grade</td><td>Steeper slopes, generally above 10 percent</td><td>Gentle to moderate slopes, generally under 10 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Walkability</td><td>Requires stepping up or down at intervals</td><td>Continuous, no steps to navigate</td></tr><tr><td>Drainage handling</td><td>Easier to manage water at each landing</td><td>Requires careful cross-slope grading</td></tr><tr><td>Construction complexity</td><td>Higher, needs risers and landings</td><td>Moderate, needs careful base grading</td></tr><tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Harder for wheelchairs, strollers, wheelbarrows</td><td>Better for wheeled access if graded correctly</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A rough way to think about it: if your slope rises more than about one foot for every ten feet of walkway length, a stepped design is usually safer and more comfortable to use. Below that, a gradually sloped walkway can often be built without steps, as long as the grading and drainage are handled correctly.</p>
<h2><b>Why Slopes Complicate a Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<p>On flat ground, water sits where it lands unless you grade it away deliberately. On a slope, water is already moving, and it will keep moving through, under, and around your walkway unless you plan for where it’s supposed to go. This is the core difference that drives almost every decision in this guide. A poorly planned sloped walkway doesn’t fail because the pavers were laid wrong, it fails because water found a path underneath or beside the base that nobody accounted for.</p>
<p>This is also why cost, base depth, and material choice all behave a bit differently on a slope than they do on flat ground, which is worth understanding before you start pricing out the project.</p>
<h2><b>Measuring Your Slope Before You Decide on an Approach</b></h2>
<h3><b>Assess the Actual Grade First</b></h3>
<p>Before deciding on an approach, measure the slope using a level and a straight board, or a laser level for a longer walkway. Knowing your actual grade, not just an estimate by eye, determines whether a gradual approach is realistic or whether you’re really looking at a stepped design. To measure by hand, hold one end of a straight board at the top of the slope and a level on top of it, then measure the height at the low end once the board reads level. Divide that height by the board’s length to get your percentage grade.</p>
<h3><b>Understanding What the Numbers Mean</b></h3>
<p>A 5 percent grade is barely noticeable underfoot and works well for a gradual walkway. A 10 percent grade starts to feel like a noticeable incline, and is generally the upper limit for a no-step design without adding traction texture to the paver surface. Anything steeper than that is where steps typically become the safer and more comfortable choice.</p>
<h2><b>Building a Gradual Sloped Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Excavate With the Slope, Not Against It</b></h3>
<p>Unlike a flat walkway, excavation on a slope needs to follow the existing grade rather than flattening it out completely. The goal is a consistent, gentle slope along the walkway’s length, typically no steeper than what’s comfortable to walk on without extra traction, generally under an 8 to 10 percent grade for a no-step design.</p>
<h3><b>Build a Deeper, More Compacted Base</b></h3>
<p>Sloped walkways benefit from a slightly deeper base than flat ones, since the base has more work to do resisting gravity and water movement. Compact in thinner layers than you would on flat ground, checking that each layer holds firm before adding the next, since a base that isn’t fully compacted on a slope tends to creep downhill over time rather than staying put. Our<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> guide to proper paver base depth</a> covers base fundamentals in more detail, though sloped applications generally call for extra depth and more careful layer by layer compaction beyond what’s needed on flat ground.</p>
<h3><b>Grade Across the Walkway, Not Just Along It</b></h3>
<p>In addition to the downhill slope, the walkway needs a slight cross-slope, a gentle tilt from one side to the other, so water sheds off to one side rather than running straight down the center of the path. This cross-slope is easy to miss if you’re only thinking about the uphill to downhill grade.</p>
<h3><b>Use Edge Restraints Rated for Slope Applications</b></h3>
<p>Standard edge restraints can work on gentle slopes, but steeper gradual slopes benefit from restraints anchored more frequently and, in some cases, backed by a small buried curb of compacted base material to prevent the whole installation from creeping downhill after repeated freeze and thaw cycles.</p>
<h3><b>Choose Pavers With Texture for Traction</b></h3>
<p>On any slope, even a gentle one, a smooth, polished paver surface becomes noticeably more slippery when wet or icy than it would on flat ground. Textured or tumbled paver finishes provide meaningfully better footing on a grade, and it’s worth prioritizing traction over a perfectly smooth look for any walkway with a noticeable incline.</p>

